{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3244","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3244","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3244\",\"parent_revision\":\"9eb1f6bcbedece679af97aa170e63afaf253675f05e2d89f28aa863cb7deb0d1\",\"root_revision\":\"9eb1f6bcbedece679af97aa170e63afaf253675f05e2d89f28aa863cb7deb0d1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-27\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nprocess-siren (plugin-creator:process-siren agent / process-siren:woo-sailor skill) produces Mermaid diagrams inline in the target file under a fidelity mandate \u2014 one node per prose step/condition/outcome, nothing merged or omitted. When the target is a skill's SKILL.md, a large diagram can push the file's token count over skilllint's SK006/AS005 threshold (4400 tokens), triggering a warning that must then be resolved by hand.\\n\\nObserved: running woo-sailor's full-step-fidelity rebuild against plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md's Dispatch Flow diagram (PR #3243) pushed the file from 4306 to 4977 tokens, over the threshold. A separate follow-up agent then had to manually relocate the diagram to a references/dispatch-flow.md file behind a context pointer to clear the warning \u2014 this repo's existing progressive-disclosure convention (mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents) already supports this pattern, and references/acceptance-test-guide.md in the same skill is a working precedent.\\n\\nprocess-siren should make this decision itself instead of requiring a manual follow-up every time: when a diagram it produces is reference/verification material that some execution paths don't need (information-hierarchy tier 3), and inlining it would push the target file over the token-budget threshold, it should extract the diagram to a disclosed reference file reached by a context pointer, rather than always inlining. When a diagram is load-bearing content every execution path needs (information-hierarchy tier 1), it should stay inline regardless of size \u2014 a blanket always-extract-when-large rule would incorrectly push genuinely load-bearing diagrams behind a pointer that every invocation needs to see immediately.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: Tie the extraction threshold to skilllint's own 4400-token SK006/AS005 line (or a diagram-specific line count). Update the plugin-creator:process-siren agent definition and/or the process-siren:woo-sailor skill's dispatch logic to make this decision automatically. The extracted file should follow the existing references/*.md + context-pointer pattern already used elsewhere in this repo, not a bare .mmd/.mermaid file.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 surfaced while running the multi-perspective-review skill-tightening pipeline (PRs #3240-#3243)\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-27\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Note\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T00:58:02.950912Z</sub>\\n\\nprocess-siren's purpose is a process clarifier and communicator for the reading agent, not a diagram-drawing agent \u2014 it already does the harder work first (analyzes the whole picture before drawing, follows workflow/graph design methodology when it does draw, then validates Mermaid syntax and layout). That framing should drive the inline-vs-disclose decision this item asks for: the trigger is not simply \\\"the diagram is large\\\" or \\\"the file crossed a token threshold\\\" \u2014 it is whether keeping the diagram inline still serves process clarity for the agent that has to follow it, versus whether disclosure would serve clarity better (or at least not harm it) while relieving token pressure. A load-bearing diagram every execution path needs stays inline even if large, because inlining IS what serves clarity there; a diagram that is reference/verification material only some paths consult is a stronger disclosure candidate on clarity grounds alone, and the token threshold is corroborating evidence, not the primary trigger.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"f7afe8d292360224f544a06fe8d888fb993bc51b71a94653572f65c6856b6eff\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQ9QcZA\"}"}